export abstract anchor FactoryAuthoring:
    packagingMechanics:
        Consuming projects obtain the factory specification through ordinary
        Piton packaging. They tether the package and update it with the normal
        Piton commands. No separate factory-specific update or drift command
        is mandated. The reconciliation required after a tethered update still
        applies through FactoryAdoption, and agents may compare the project's
        implementation with the updated specification as part of that work.
    scaffoldCapability:
        The package ships a scaffold capability as a Belay skill. A consuming
        project uses the skill before any project-specific factory exists, so
        no factory executable is needed beforehand. The skill guides an agent
        to create the initial implementation from the tethered specification
        in the engineer's chosen technology. It does not fork or depend on the
        reference implementation.
    projectSpecificConfiguration:
        Configuration remains project-specific. The descriptor records where it
        lives, but the package prescribes no universal configuration schema or
        language. Durable, non-secret decisions belong in project-owned Piton;
        runtime settings and credential references belong in the
        implementation's own configuration. Credentials are never stored in the
        specification.
    scaffoldOutput:
        The scaffold produces a runnable minimal core. That core includes the
        descriptor, a configuration file, all six core commands, a minimal
        dashboard meeting the dashboard contract, the wiring directory,
        project-owned Piton for durable non-secret decisions, run instructions,
        and a verification report. Optional commands are added only when
        requested. Mandatory test scaffolding is not required. The scaffold does
        not implement the full capability catalog; the project grows only the
        capabilities it needs, following FactoryAdoption.
    scaffoldTechnology:
        The scaffold is agent-driven and supports any stack it can implement and
        validate. The package declares a baseline stack for conformance, Python
        3.12 with SQLite and a local HTTP dashboard; projects may choose another
        stack. An incapable agent reports unsupported rather than producing a
        partial factory.
    scaffoldInstallation:
        Preflight checks every target path. It refuses on existing files, an
        existing `.factory` directory without a descriptor, symlink escape, or a
        concurrent marker. It stages writes and moves them atomically, writing
        the descriptor last. A crash marker supports resume or clean failure and
        never leaves a half-scaffold that blocks retry.
    scaffoldValidation:
        Validation runs in an isolated temporary workspace with stubbed external
        services, no real credentials, and a cost cap. Onboard validates
        configuration and policy. Status and shutdown run on empty state. Submit
        creates a visible order. Start uses a stub or local adapter and validated
        launch wiring; if dispatch cannot be validated, the report says
        accepted-not-dispatched rather than claiming dispatch. Answer uses a
        synthetic question injected into the sandbox. The dashboard is loaded and
        checked against its monitoring and core-action contract. Persistence is
        checked across two invocations.
        The report records technology, files, commands verified, commands
        unverified with reasons, dashboard result, and unresolved decisions.
